  "read": {
    "headline": "The ten-year spiked to 5.365% and ended the week flat.",
    "standfirst": "Weekend edition. The week's story in rates was a round trip: the ten-year touched 5.349% on Monday and 5.365% on Wednesday, its highest since 2002, then closed Thursday at 5.22% against 5.24% a week earlier, with the two-year at 4.75% against 4.78%, after three auctions that each cleared at the highest yield in years (the thirty-year at 5.618% on Thursday) and drew enough demand to pull rates back. In equities the S&P 500 gained 0.55% on the week and the Nasdaq Composite was flat, while the Russell 2000 lost 1.37%; the Nasdaq closed at records on Monday and Tuesday and the S&P 500 on Tuesday, then both fell on Wednesday, and on Thursday the Nasdaq lost 1.25%, its biggest fall since mid-August, after the Financial Times reported that OpenAI's annualised revenue is about $20 billion below what investors were told. Brent rose about 4% on Thursday on tanker attacks and a Gulf hurricane, then fell on Friday after the president said the US will not strike Iran before the 3 November midterms. Gold gained 1.5% on the week, Bitcoin fell 4.7% and Korea's Kospi lost 5.4%, with Korea and Taiwan shut today. Ahead: Michigan sentiment today, the banks and CPI next week, and the Fed on 28 October.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The week in the curve: the ten-year touched 5.365% and ended 2bp lower than it began.",
        "text": "The two-year closed Thursday at 4.75%, the ten-year at 5.22% and the thirty-year at 5.60%, against 4.78%, 5.24% and 5.61% a week earlier. The ten-year touched 5.349% on Monday after the ISM services prices-paid reading of 74.0 and 5.365% on Wednesday, and the thirty-year about 5.73%, both the highest since 2002. The three-year auction cleared at 4.932% on Tuesday, the highest since 2006, with a bid-to-cover of 2.62; the ten-year at 5.300% on Wednesday, the highest since 2000, with 2.77; and the thirty-year at 5.618% on Thursday, with 2.54 against a recent average near 2.4. The Thursday rally was led by the long end, with the 20-year-plus Treasury fund up 0.94% on the day and still down 4.72% over a month. The minutes of the September meeting said most participants see another hike as likely appropriate by year-end; futures price October as a hold at about 80%, a December hike at about 70% and another in March at about 45%, and Waller said more hikes are needed but need not come immediately."
      },
      {
        "title": "Records Monday and Tuesday, then a Nasdaq drop of 1.25% on Thursday over OpenAI's revenue.",
        "text": "The S&P 500 gained 0.55% on the week to 7,765.36, the Nasdaq Composite was flat at 27,193.34 and the Dow gained 0.11% to 51,231.64, while the Russell 2000 lost 1.37%. Both the S&P 500 and the Nasdaq closed at records on Tuesday, then fell on Wednesday, and on Thursday the Nasdaq lost 1.25%, the biggest fall since mid-August, after the Financial Times reported that OpenAI's annualised revenue was approaching $50 billion at the end of September against $70 billion reported by investors; Oracle fell about 5.5%, Nvidia 2.9% and AMD 3.9%, and the Philadelphia Semiconductor index fell about 3.8% in the afternoon. The damage was narrow: more than two-thirds of S&P 500 members rose, energy gained 2.97% and consumer staples 2.11%, and technology fell 1.79%. Over the week energy gained 3.85%, staples 3.59% and utilities 3.11%, while technology lost 1.02% and industrials 0.91%; momentum lagged the S&P 500 by 2.28% and small-cap growth by 2.60%."
      },
      {
        "title": "Brent rose 4% on Thursday and fell 1.4% on Friday after the president ruled out strikes before the midterms.",
        "text": "December Brent is $102.83, down 1.39% this morning and up 0.57% on the week, and WTI $90.29, down 1.31% and 0.90% lower on the week. Brent settled near $104.3 on Thursday, up about 4%, after reports that the Pentagon had prepared plans for three days of strikes on Iranian targets, nine tanker attacks near Hormuz in a week, and a hurricane that led Gulf of Mexico producers to shut in about 1.3 million barrels a day, according to FXStreet. It fell on Friday after the president said the US would not attack Iran before the 3 November midterm elections and that talks were productive, while keeping the naval blockade of Iranian ports; Vice President Vance said Iran must cut its enrichment capacity, and Iran's atomic energy chief rejected giving it up. Heating oil is $4.82, up 7.00% on the week, and natural gas up 3.36%. The Brent 5D column now lines up with WTI's, but its 1M column, +1.60% against WTI's −6.00%, still straddles the 30 September roll."
      },
      {
        "title": "Europe fell 0.9% on the week, France worst. Lagarde said the ECB has tools if markets turn disorderly.",
        "text": "The Stoxx 600 closed Thursday at 625.51, down 0.93% on the week, with the DAX 1.7% lower on the week, the CAC 40 2.1% and Italy's FTSE MIB 2.4%, while the FTSE 100 was down 0.2%. French ten-year yields touched 4.92% in the week and the premium over Germany has recently posted its biggest weekly jump in decades; euro-area finance ministers and the ECB told France to pass a 2027 budget, the Bank of France's governor said the spike was France's problem, and Bloomberg reported that Lagarde told ministers the ECB can counter disorderly moves if certain criteria are met. The euro is $1.12, up 0.64% on the week after touching a 16-month low near $1.116 on Monday, and the dollar index is near 102.3; the dollar is on course for a fourth straight weekly gain, according to Investing.com. One estimate has the market pricing about two and a half more ECB hikes, from three and a half at the end of September, and the accounts of the 10 September meeting came out on Thursday."
      },
      {
        "title": "Korea lost 5.4% on the week and is shut today. Brazil gained 7.3%. India hiked.",
        "text": "The Kospi fell 2.62% on Thursday to 6,625.93 and 5.39% on the week, the Korea ETF 8.12%, with Samsung Electronics guiding to a record third-quarter operating profit but foreign investors selling; the Kospi and Taiwan's market are shut today for holidays. The Nikkei is down 0.24% near 68,878, Hong Kong up 1.09% and Shanghai down 1.21% after reopening from its week-long holiday, with Singapore's ETF down 4.07% on the week. Brazil's Bovespa gained 7.34% on the week to 206,220 and the Brazil ETF 11.52%, after Monday's election-driven surge; the real is 5.01. India's central bank raised its repo rate to 5.5% on Wednesday and the Sensex is up 0.9% this morning, with the India ETF down 2.06% on the week. Emerging-market stocks fell 2.32% on the week and 1.89% on Thursday."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold hit a weekly high of $4,223. Crypto lost 5–11% on the week. Orange juice gained 11%.",
        "text": "Gold is $4,222.80, up 1.58% this morning and 1.45% on the week, after touching a two-month low near $4,066 on Wednesday; silver is $60.80, up 2.32%, and platinum up 2.45%. The dollar's softer tone and Thursday's drop in Treasury yields supported the metals, while gold is still down 5.33% over a month. Bitcoin is $82,437, up 0.91% this morning and down 4.71% on the week, Ether down 8.54%, Solana 9.06% and Dogecoin 10.84%. In agriculture orange juice gained 10.91% on the week, oats 8.31% and soybean meal 4.48%, while sugar fell 3.26% this morning and is up 9.51% over a month, and lean hogs are down 17.42% over a month and 30.71% over three."
      }
    ]
